A||||20||ChadMan||Uh oh - wiggly steering wheel||||11
Z||000000||ChadMan||11-01-1999||07:35 PM||||Within the past 3000 miles, my 88 Coupe has developed a "wiggle" in the tilt steering column.  I'm not talking about play in the steering (there's hardly any) - I'm talking about the entire column from the tilt-joint up.  You can grab the wheel and move it back and forth - only about an eighth of an inch, but still, it's kind of unnerving.  The rest of the column (below the tilt joint) doesn't wiggle.  My 86 SE does not have any wiggle at all in its column.<P>What do you guys think?  Is this a safety issue?  Can I fix the original column, or do I need to replace it?  Thanks.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Terrybogin||11-01-1999||08:40 PM||||Yes, it can be fixed. Is it an easy fix??? NO WAY! You really get into the column to tighten up the loose bolts. I have found that it is easier to just swap in another column because the ones that I fixed came loose again later and had to be retightened again. ||||reg||13
Z||000002||superXmotorsports||11-01-1999||09:09 PM||||Terrybogin - Is it really easier to just swap the entire steering column rather than tighten the bolts?  I have the same problem and had planned to swap columns until someone told me to simply tighten the bolts.  So now I am confused.  I would appreciate additional opinions on this.<BR>Thanks!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000003||HotFieroGT||11-01-1999||11:26 PM||||I had the same problem a few years ago, my ex loved to hot rod my red 88GT and I think she hit something hard and the steering column came loose and would flop around as you were driving.  I took the column apart and sure enough the bolts were loose.  You will need a steering wheel puller.  I think I took the column completely out of the car and I remember having to take it to a dealership and borrow a special tool to completely disassemble it, I remember there being something inside I couldn't get loose without that tool.  If you do go this route be sure to loctite the bolts, it is definately something you don't want to do more than once!<p>[This message has been edited by HotFieroGT (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000004||terryk||11-02-1999||01:52 AM||||The primary cause of the loose steering wheel is allowing the wheel to bang against the stops when you pull the tilt handle. If you want to keep it tight, hold the steering wheel, pull the tilt lever and then raise it to the top. Don't just pull the tilt lever and let it swing up.<P>I've tightened more wheel for this reason. If you do take it apart, apply Loc-tite to the screws.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||JIM GOOSE||11-02-1999||08:38 AM||||I had this problem on my GMC Jimmy and it got looser & looser until I thought the wheel was going to come off. You will need to go in and fix it at some point & it is BITCHY. You need to be careful and take note of all the parts relationship to each other so you can return to original places.  Along the way, also had to make a couple of special little tools. When you get down into it, its 4 screws that get loose and you will want to LOCTITE for sure. THIS IS SOMETHING THAT SHOULD NEVER HAPPEN & IT IS GM'S FAULTY ENGR & ASSEMBLY THAT IS THE PROBLEM !!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||JIM GOOSE||11-02-1999||08:38 AM||||I had this problem on my GMC Jimmy and it got looser & looser until I thought the wheel was going to come off. You will need to go in and fix it at some point & it is BITCHY. You need to be careful and take note of all the parts relationship to each other so you can return to original places.  Along the way, also had to make a couple of special little tools. When you get down into it, its 4 screws that get loose and you will want to LOCTITE for sure. THIS IS SOMETHING THAT SHOULD NEVER HAPPEN & IT IS GM'S FAULTY ENGR & ASSEMBLY THAT IS THE PROBLEM !!!||||reg||8
Z||000007||ChadMan||11-02-1999||09:37 AM||||Thanks for everyone's input...(big sigh)  OK, it sounds like I'm going to have to fix this.  When I pull the steering wheel, will the bolts I need to tighten be immediately visible?  Or will I have to dig down and keep removing "guts" from the column?  Any helpful tips?||||reg||8
Z||000008||theogre||11-02-1999||12:28 PM||||replace the column.<P>If you want a bit of an idea what's involved go to the <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl" TARGET=_blank>ogre cave</A> area and read about the column there. If you think the discription is long wait untill you actually try it. Even with the tools it takes me 2-4 hours to do this job.<P>from now on Don't let your tilt unit slam.||||reg||1
Z||000009||JIM GOOSE||11-02-1999||12:50 PM||||Let me check my notes on the repair I did and the tools that were used. Assuming you have the same problem, you'll need to pull the switch, turn signals etc and the screws are not easy to get at when down there.||||reg||8
Z||000010||lowCG||11-02-1999||01:39 PM||||Ugh,now I have another,head-spinning,all Saturday task to accomplish,but it's real good to find out that the cause has been determined.<BR>Any way to put a chunk of rubber somewhere to prevent this,or will loctite(red)take care of things?||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||11-02-1999||03:01 PM||||Holding the steering wheel when you pull the tilt lever is the solution. It can be done with one hand. <P>Since I usually tilt it fully up only when I get out of the car,I put my hand through the wheel, press down on the spoke with my arm, pull the level and let the wheel up gently.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||11-02-1999||08:09 PM||||You can get to two of the offending bolts without disassembling the entire column. But to get to all four (something I have never been able to do) it would appear to really be a pain. Last time this happened to me, I just swapped in another column and did not let the wheel spring up when releasing the tilt. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wolfhound||11-02-1999||08:58 PM||||I have the same problem thats occured in the last few days. I have never let the column  fly up unrestrained. I very seldom even change it. I think it,s a design flaw. <BR> I,m going to take a shot at fixing it. I may be a glutton for punishment but I,ll learn something in the process. <BR> I took a fifty mile trip and started with a solid wheel and finished loose as a goose.<BR> I have a parts car with a column and if I can,t pull off the fix, I,ll swap. Whats to loose but 4 hours most folks would have spent watching TV. ||||reg||7
Z||000014||Scott2M4||11-02-1999||10:19 PM||||Well, I just wasted bandwidth by posting this very same question a few minutes ago.  Sorry, folks.  I guess I really _SHOULD_ read before posting!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>OK - so, if I have to pull the wheel to do this repair, I guess it's a good excuse to swap in a new wheel!<P>Well, which of you has a slightly used GT wheel that you're willing to part with!?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000015||JIM GOOSE||11-03-1999||09:19 AM||||I would say 4hrs on this job is only if you have done it before & have the special tools needed to go all the way into the column. Along the way in this repair, I made 3 special tools that took at least 4 hrs for me to devise and fabricate. You really need the factory shop manual for the drawings of the column and the tools in the first place. The puller used on the pivot pins is a device straight from hell that I had to upgrade twice before having a useful, functional tool. I'm going to take a picture of these tools & put on my site at <A HREF="http://www.hamptonmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.hamptonmotorsports.com</A>  very shortly. I will say that this a job not for everyone & not a hammer and pliers deal at all. There is a ball bearing in the column that is free to release all of its tiny balls if not handled carefully as an example. This repair is similar to taking apart & reasembling a windup clock (LOL) except its at the heart of that vehicle you need to use.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||11-03-1999||09:44 AM||||Cooter, that's why I said replace the column. Those other 2 bolts must be tightened or you risk fracturing the tilt unit. You have to completely strip the column and take apart the tilt unit itself.<P>I've been doing this stuff for a long time. I won't even waste my time with this project again unless there is no source of replacement columns. Since the Fiero column is common to other small GM cars, that's not really likely to be a problem for many people.<P>The tilt pivot pins are a giant pain. you can't pry them out. if you try you'll likely break the frame. the bearings are just as big a pain. Spill that thing and your likely never to find all the bb's.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Scott2M4||11-03-1999||03:09 PM||||OK.  For future reference (for those future owners that'll be searching the archives), how about someone submitting prices for new columns.<P>I, for one, would like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Scott2M4||11-03-1999||03:09 PM||||OK.  For future reference (for those future owners that'll be searching the archives), how about someone submitting prices for new columns.<P>I, for one, would like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Scott2M4||11-03-1999||03:09 PM||||OK.  For future reference (for those future owners that'll be searching the archives), how about someone submitting prices for new columns.<P>I, for one, would like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Patrick||11-03-1999||11:00 PM||||<BR>Talking about archives, I'm surprised that no one brought up the fact that we just had a similar thread to this one a little while ago.<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000880.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/000880.html</A> <P>As usual, there was a difference of opinion on how to deal with this issue. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
